Cell[TextData[{
 "The chain has two rank-2 boundary tensors at the ends and four rank-3 bulk \
tensors in the middle, with bond indices linking neighbors and one free \
physical leg per site. The bond indices form a hyperedge between two adjacent \
tensors, the physical legs are free legs that are not contracted. Reading the \
network's ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["\"\<Hyperedges\>\"", TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  ExpressionUUID->"d1c18e14-2a3b-45b4-8d93-e5b65e9d70c0"],
 " property confirms this: the first six entries on the integer index list \
are bond indices linking sites 1-2, 2-3 and so on, and the next six are the \
physical indices unique to each site. A tensor-ring (periodic) topology is \
requested with ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"\"\<Boundary\>\"", "\[Rule]", "\"\<Periodic\>\""}], 
   TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "5130c8b9-0ddb-45e3-b46f-cdff385ebee4"],
 "; that variant promotes the boundary tensors to rank-3 by giving the first \
and last sites an extra bond index that links them, closing the chain into a \
ring. Tensor rings are useful for thermodynamic-limit calculations and \
translationally invariant ansatze, but the algorithms documented in the \
remainder of this tutorial \[LongDash] canonicalization, overlap, Schmidt \
decomposition and truncation \[LongDash] target the open-boundary layout. For \
periodic chains, contract the ring directly or transform to an open MPS with \
a boundary virtual leg before invoking these primitives."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"7bb0fe7e-0363-4ca6-b56e-5827baa557e5"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["2. Canonical forms", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"750724cd-9ddc-4aa5-842d-db4b53143b8e"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
 "An MPS has a gauge freedom: inserting any invertible matrix and its inverse \
on a bond does not change the contracted state but does change the individual \
site tensors. This redundancy is not a bug; it is the structural reason \
algorithms like DMRG can converge quickly and stably. Canonical forms fix the \
gauge in a controlled way, putting the chain into a state where one site \
holds the wavefunction's spectral content and every other site is an \
isometry. Algorithms then operate on the small spectral block instead of the \
whole chain. Different gauges of an MPS represent the same physical state but \
expose different structure for downstream algorithms. The function ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["MPSCanonicalForm",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/MPSCanonicalForm"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"0de0e99d-547f-4e87-97df-c04cdb02c622"],
 " produces three of them. The ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["\"\<Left\>\"", TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "16bac8ed-3383-4deb-a686-0edc4b3c6501"],
 " form makes every site tensor left-isometric, meaning the matrix obtained \
by grouping its left bond and physical leg satisfies ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox["A", "\[Dagger]"], ".", "A", "=", "I"}], TraditionalForm]],
   "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"b782ab7f-9949-4b7e-8744-efcc68dcf735"],
 ". The ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["\"\<Right\>\"", TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "f74fd83d-d866-4297-8218-efd8cccaae89"],
 " form is the mirror image: every site is right-isometric, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"A", ".", 
    SuperscriptBox["A", "\[Dagger]"], "=", "I"}], TraditionalForm]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"fdd56ec9-a12f-4eda-91f1-f2d7d3cab8e6"],
 ". The mixed form ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"\"\<Mixed\>\"", ",", "k"}], "}"}], TraditionalForm]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"7c79c71c-3207-450c-9355-759e80188128"],
 " is left-canonical for sites strictly below ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["k", TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "204c9ef8-b85a-4824-b016-1bb3c94c9236"],
 " and right-canonical for sites strictly above ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["k", TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "29a0a6fa-5d2b-4e2e-9a77-04af06fb8b65"],
 "; site ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["k", TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "0cf50e21-7347-4261-a3f9-4d38e9f5838b"],
 " is the orthogonality center carrying the wavefunction's spectral weight \
and is the local site updated by DMRG. Internally each form is produced by a \
sweep of singular value decompositions: a left sweep absorbs the residual ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"S", "\[CenterDot]", 
    SuperscriptBox["V", "\[Dagger]"]}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  ExpressionUUID->"fe6e4673-3473-4ab2-a07f-fe644a04120d"],
 " factor into the next tensor and keeps ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["U", TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "2f9372ce-5a1b-471d-bf66-ee9444586c29"],
 " as the new left-isometric site, and the right sweep is the transpose of \
this procedure. The orthogonality center after one full sweep sits at the \
last site, which is why ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["\"\<Left\>\"", TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "c4efc9a8-2042-4d43-8def-db99db45c6dd"],
 " is equivalent to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"\"\<Mixed\>\"", ",", "N"}], "}"}], TraditionalForm]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"4d977ac9-c08d-4882-8737-1444ce2ab6ba"],
 "."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"d0798652-477c-4455-a1ca-8ddadad4c472"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"canon", "=", 
  RowBox[{"MPSCanonicalForm", "[", 
   RowBox[{"mps", ",", "\"\<Left\>\""}], "]"}]}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.988116394399743*^9, 3.988116395221869*^9}},
 CellLabel->
  "In[209]:=",ExpressionUUID->"8f2426fd-6f69-4a2d-8c75-3e2a0c75da69"],

